      "created_at": "2018-05-25T01:59:59Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-05-25T02:17:20Z",
      "body": "@stefa98 You will need to first disable automatic init the OneSignal WordPress does. \r\n\r\n1. At the bottom of the OneSignal WordPress settings you will need to toggle on \"Disable OneSignal initialization\".\r\n\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/645861/40521376-244a6a8a-5f80-11e8-9ab9-73eb786f0687.png)\r\n\r\n2. Then you can initialize OneSignal through javascript with the `requiresUserPrivacyConsent` setting.\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<script src=\"https://cdn.onesignal.com/sdks/OneSignalSDK.js\" async></script>\r\n<script>\r\n  var OneSignal = window.OneSignal || [];\r\n  window._oneSignalInitOptions[\"requiresUserPrivacyConsent\"] = true;\r\n  OneSignal.push([\"init\", window._oneSignalInitOptions]);\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n`window._oneSignalInitOptions` contains all the settings from the WordPress plugin including app_id are already set in here.\r\n\r\n3. When the user accepts your privacy agreement you will need to call `userTappedProvideConsentButton`\r\n```javascript\r\nfunction userTappedProvideConsentButton() {\r\n  // Will initialize the SDK and register for push notifications\r\n  OneSignal.push(function() {\r\n    OneSignal.provideUserConsent(true);\r\n  });\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n",
